WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.728
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of "Cloud Performance Atlas,"

00:00:02.728 --> 00:00:04.680
I show how to extend
tracing to every aspect

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.600
of your cloud-native
application.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.910
Will our performance
stay inside the lines?

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:09.930
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.760
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.750
One of my favorite things
about managed services like App

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.342
Engine is that your requests
are automatically traced,

00:00:18.342 --> 00:00:19.800
letting you look
at a given request

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.690
and see where various
execution time is

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.780
going without you having
to write any code yourself.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:26.280
Sadly, if you
wanted the same sort

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.470
of data for your
non-managed services,

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.750
it meant a lot of custom
code, custom deployments,

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:34.410
and a whole new team to write
a custom dashboard UI for you.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:35.940
But not any more.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.070
Stackdriver has recently
released a library

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.690
which allows your
non-managed code

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.790
to get the same tracing
awesomeness with less

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:42.840
hassle to you.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.430
Using the newly created
OpenCensus library,

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.390
you can get instrumentation
data from any VMs or containers

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.250
running in your application
across a number of languages

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:55.200
including Java, Python,
Node.js, Golang and more.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.840
Heck, you could even run it on
your non-Google cloud services

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.370
and get that data, too.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:02.430
The client-side library works
by patching well-known modules

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:05.340
in your application in order
to label and measure latency

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.650
of RPCs and incoming requests.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.660
And for the not so
well-known functions,

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:12.900
you can also add custom
spans to your code

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.316
that catch the important
bits that fall between.

00:01:15.316 --> 00:01:17.190
All that data is then
collected and displayed

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.800
in a nice, consistent
UI in the Cloud Console

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.530
to help you track down where
your performance is going.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.390
If you're interested in getting
started with Stackdriver

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.723
Tracing, check out the article
linked in the description

00:01:26.723 --> 00:01:29.010
below, and don't forget to
subscribe to our YouTube

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.679
channel for more great "Cloud
Performance Atlas" content.

00:01:31.679 --> 00:01:33.220
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:34.440
every millisecond counts.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

